//
// loft#2043 — A MOVED GENERATOR IS NOT READ (`@FR-G-Hold`, `@FR-H-Move`, `@FR-H-Spent`).  A
// generator local given to a field, an element or a tuple member MOVES there (G-Hold), so the
// name is spent and reading it is a compile-time error that names where the value went.  It
// compiled: `next(g)` drove the frame its holder now owned (1, 2), and once the holder released
// it (`v.remove(0)`) answered null, silently, on both backends.  One file per position, since a
// refusal stops the file: this one is the append; the field, literal, tuple and returned
// literal are the sibling files.  The legal half — a refill, a move on one path only, a view, a
// parameter's own hold, a call argument — is in
// `a-generator-moved-into-a-holder-is-released-once-by-it.loft` and the cells beside it.
//
// @falsified-at: d1bff0fb0 — hand-measured, interpret and native: the control compiles this file
//   and prints `1 2` then `null`.
// CHANNEL: the expected diagnostic.
// ARMED: no instrument — the refusal is on by default.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names the moved name and the line it moved at — once per reading
//   line, so the read before the `remove` and the one after it are two expected errors.
// HOLDS: the same read of a moved droppable is refused on both trees.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `g` is read after its value moved
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `g` is read after its value moved

fn counter() -> iterator<integer> { for i in 1..100 { yield i; } }

fn main() {
  g = counter();
  v: vector<iterator<integer>> = [];
  v += [g];
  println("{next(g)} {next(g)}");
  v.remove(0);
  println("{next(g)}");
}
